course: dictionary
lesson: 2392
title: Money words: currency
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every country has its own money, and that money has a name. That name is its currency. «Every country chooses its own money, called its currency.» So when you meet the word currency, ask yourself which money the sentence is talking about.
explain: The context clues show you the meaning. «Ukraine has the hryvnia (₴), China the yuan (¥), Japan the yen (¥), Nigeria the naira, the USA the dollar and the UK the pound.» Each country has a different currency, so a currency is the kind of money a country uses.
explain: Currency can also mean money in general, as coins and notes. In «About twenty European countries, including Ireland, France and Spain, share one currency, the euro», the word names one particular money that many countries share. Try swapping in your meaning: the countries share one kind of money, the euro. It still makes sense.
fact: A currency is the kind of money a country uses, such as the dollar, the pound or the euro.
fact: Currency can also mean money in general, as coins and notes.
fact: «Currency» is a noun, and its plural is currencies.
fact: The euro (€) is shared by many European countries, including Ireland, France and Spain.
fact: A currency is different from a country's name: Japan's currency is the yen (¥), not Japan.
exercise: «A currency is the kind of money a country uses.» What is a currency? | *The kind of money a country uses | A country's flag | A bank card
exercise: «Japan the yen (¥)» is in a sentence about money. Which currency does Japan use? | *The yen | The naira | The dollar | The euro
exercise: Which of these is a currency? | A passport | A shop | A bank | *The pound
test: «Every country chooses its own money, called its currency.» What does currency mean in this sentence? | A map of the country | *The money a country uses | A country's border | A foreign holiday
test: Ella is going to Spain, and she wants to change her pounds into euros. What is an exchange rate for? | *To change one currency into another | To name a country | To send a letter abroad | To count coins at home
exam: In «most of them even share one currency, the euro», what does currency mean? | *A shared kind of money, the euro | A shared border | A shared language | A shared football team
